Subject: [PATCH] ggml-zendnn: set the size gate from measurement on non-VNNI hosts

The adaptive fallback declines any matmul with 128 tokens or fewer. That is
a reasonable rule where AOCL-DLP does the work, and it was a correct one for
BullDNN too when it was written -- the native decode path then ran at a tenth
of ggml's rate.

It is no longer correct. The decode GEMV reads GGML's packed blocks directly
and now beats ggml, and the GEMM beats it from four tokens up. Under the old
rule none of that is reachable: token generation is one token and never
dispatches, and llama-bench's pp128 misses the threshold by one.

Measured on an FX-8370E (bdver2, no AVX2, so ggml is on SSSE3 as well),
m=4096 k=14336, ggml against native, two passes each:

    tokens    1      2      4      8      512
    q4_K   1.16x  0.65x  1.02x  1.12x   1.55x
    q5_K   1.28x  0.87x  1.21x  1.70x   2.15x

So two and three tokens are the only counts worth declining: too many rows
for the GEMV, which handles one, and the GEMM pads M to its tile of four and
throws half the work away. Everything else is at least par.

The rule is applied only where BullDNN's native kernels are what runs, which
is decided by AVX-512 VNNI: with it the work goes to AOCL-DLP and the
original gate is untouched, without it the measured rule applies. So this
changes nothing on any Zen part.

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-zendnn/ggml-zendnn.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-zendnn/ggml-zendnn.cpp
@@ -759,6 +759,14 @@
     GGML_UNUSED(max_tensor_size);
 }

+// AVX-512 VNNI decides WHO runs a per-group INT8 matmul: with it, AOCL-DLP;
+// without it, BullDNN's native 128-bit kernels. The two have different
+// crossovers, so the size gate below has to know which one it is talking about.
+static bool ggml_zendnn_host_has_avx512vnni() {
+    static const bool v = __builtin_cpu_supports("avx512vnni");
+    return v;
+}
+
 static bool ggml_zendnn_adaptive_fallback_enabled() {
     static const bool enabled = std::getenv("GGML_ZENDNN_ADAPTIVE_FALLBACK") == nullptr ||
                                 std::atoi(std::getenv("GGML_ZENDNN_ADAPTIVE_FALLBACK")) != 0;
@@ -794,7 +802,33 @@
                 const int64_t K = inputs->ne[0];
                 const int64_t N = (inputs->ne[1]*inputs->ne[2]*inputs->ne[3]);
                 const int64_t M = weights->ne[1];
-                if(K <= 256 || N <= 128 || M <= 96) {
+
+                // The N > 128 rule assumes ZenDNN loses below a large batch.
+                // That holds where AOCL-DLP does the work, but on a host
+                // without AVX-512 VNNI the work is done by BullDNN's native
+                // 128-bit kernels, whose crossover is elsewhere. Measured on an
+                // FX-8370E (bdver2, no AVX2, so ggml is on SSSE3 too),
+                // m=4096 k=14336, ggml against native, two passes each:
+                //
+                //     tokens    1      2      4      8      512
+                //     q4_K   1.16x  0.65x  1.02x  1.12x   1.55x
+                //     q5_K   1.28x  0.87x  1.21x  1.70x   2.15x
+                //
+                // One token goes to the decode GEMV, which reads the packed
+                // blocks directly and beats ggml. Four and above go to the GEMM
+                // and are at least par. Two and three fall between: too many
+                // rows for the GEMV, and the GEMM pads M to its tile of four and
+                // discards half the work. So those are the only token counts
+                // worth declining here -- N > 128 would throw away every gain
+                // above, decode included.
+                if (!ggml_zendnn_host_has_avx512vnni()) {
+                    if (K <= 256 || M <= 96) {
+                        return false;
+                    }
+                    if (N == 2 || N == 3) {
+                        return false;
+                    }
+                } else if (K <= 256 || N <= 128 || M <= 96) {
                     return false;
                 }

